-56},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"ALDH2","gene_symbol_current":"ALDH2","gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.0913036,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0545842,"nucleus_score":2.12287,"pct_ad":0.300626,"pct_control":0.279932,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":0.0743505,"donor_lfcse":0.105476,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":7.06928e-06,"degas_log2fc":-0.138965,"degas_padj":0.00906148},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"ALDH2","gene_symbol_current":"ALDH2","gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":6.43347e-24,"nucleus_log2fc":0.274079,"nucleus_score":10.2458,"pct_ad":0.175828,"pct_control":0.147156,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.678552,"donor_log2fc":0.283233,"donor_lfcse":0.167406,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0111128,"degas_log2fc":0.00115472,"degas_padj":0.970356}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","module":"magenta","kme":0.181915,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.456482,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","module":"blue","kme":0.159648,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.137528,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","module":"turquoise","kme":0.0245515,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.398013,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","module":"turquoise","kme":0.000161685,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.680759,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000111275","entrez_id":["217"],"uniprot":["P05091"],"ncbi_summary":"This protein belongs to the aldehyde dehydrogenase family of proteins. Aldehyde dehydrogenase is the second enzyme of the major oxidative pathway of alcohol metabolism. Two major liver isoforms of aldehyde dehydrogenase, cytosolic and mitochondrial, can be distinguished by their electrophoretic mobilities, kinetic properties, and subcellular localizations. Most Caucasians have two major isozymes, while approximately 50% of East Asians have the cytosolic isozyme but not the mitochondrial isozyme. A remarkably higher frequency of acute alcohol intoxication among East Asians than among Caucasians could be related to the absence of a catalytically active form of the mitochondrial isozyme. The increased exposure to acetaldehyde in individuals with the catalytically inactive form may also confer greater susceptibility to many types of cancer. This gene encodes a mitochondrial isoform, which has a low Km for acetaldehydes, and is localized in mitochondrial matrix. Alternative splicing results in multiple transcript variants encoding distinct isoforms.[provided by RefSeq, Nov 2016].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P05091","text":"Mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase with broad substrate specificity which participates in the detoxification of ethanol-derived acetaldehyde and endogenous aldehydes, including those arising from lipid peroxidation (PubMed:16956664, PubMed:22339434, PubMed:33355142, PubMed:39075523). The reaction mechanism involves a nucleophilic attack on the aldehyde substrate, resulting in formation of a thiohemiacetal intermediate. Subsequent hydride transfer to NAD+, yields a thioester intermediate which is hydrolyzed to the carboxylic acid product through activation of a water molecule by the catalytic glutamate (PubMed:19506075). In vivo, ALDH2 is required for the clearance of formaldehyde, a cytotoxic and carcinogenic endogenous metabolite that induces DNA damage (PubMed:33355142). It also catalyzes the oxidation of 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetaldehyde (DOPAL), a reactive intermediate of dopamine catabolism, to 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetate (DOPAC) (PubMed:16956664). ALDH2 may also catalyze the oxidation of 4-aminobutanal (gamma-aminobutyraldehyde) to 4-aminobutanoate (GABA), representing an alternative route for GABA biosynthesis from polyamine catabolism (By similarity). In vitro, ALDH2 catalyzes the oxidation of acrolein, malondialdehyde, and 4-hydroxynonenal (4-HNE) (PubMed:22339434). It also exhibits esterase activity that is enhanced by NAD+ but does not require it, although the physiological relevance of this activity remains unclear (PubMed:19506075). ALDH2 also bioactivates the antianginal drug nitroglycerin through denitration, generating nitric oxide (NO) by a mechanism that remains incompletely understood. The generated NO activates the soluble guanylate cyclase/cGMP signaling pathway, which in turn leads to vasorelaxation and relief of angina pectoris."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}